<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Islamic Movement of Uzbekistan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1998">1998</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Islamic Movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> was a militant Islamist group founded in 1998 by Tahir Yuldashev and Juma Namangani with the aim of overthrowing President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam-karimov">Islam Karimov</a>. Based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, it made armed incursions into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a> and Uzbekistan in 1999 and 2000. It fought alongside the Taliban after 2001, lost Namangani, and later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> before declaring allegiance to the Islamic State in 2015.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/islamic-movement-of-uzbekistan.jpg" alt="Islamic Movement of Uzbekistan" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Jihad.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in Soviet Central Asia was officially suppressed until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-afghan-war">Soviet–Afghan War</a> (1979–1989), when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a> sent to fight the Afghan mujahedin came home impressed by their opponents' zeal. One of them, the paratrooper Juma Namangani, returned to Namangan in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Fergana Valley</a> and became associated with the Islamic ideologue Tohir Yuldashev. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>'s independence in 1991 they founded a Salafi group, Adolat, which took civil authority in Namangan and enforced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a>. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam-karimov">Islam Karimov</a> at first tolerated it, but by 1992 he had outlawed it.</p><p>Yuldashev and Namangani fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>, where Namangani commanded units of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-tajik-opposition">United Tajik Opposition</a> in the civil war. Yuldashev toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and from 1995 to 1998 was based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, where he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osama-bin-laden">Osama bin Laden</a>. After the 1997 peace agreement in Tajikistan, the two men, disillusioned with the political concessions made by the Tajik Islamists, formed the Islamic Movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> in 1998 to oppose Karimov.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After explosions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> in 1999, Karimov blamed the movement, though that attribution remains disputed. Later that year it made its first verifiable raid, into the Batken region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>, seizing the Mayor of Osh and obtaining a ransom and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopter</a>. Under pressure, Namangani left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> for northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> in late 1999. In 2000 the movement, then estimated at about 2,000 strong, supplied around 600 fighters to the Taliban's offensive against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-massoud">Ahmad Shah Massoud</a>, and raided Kyrgyzstan and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> again. In August 2000 it kidnapped four American climbers, who escaped on 12 August, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> designated it a Foreign Terrorist Organization.</p><p>After the American-led invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> the movement was largely destroyed. Namangani was killed in November 2001 by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> airstrike, and Yuldashev withdrew to the tribal areas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, where he was killed in a drone strike on 27 August 2009. Fighting broke out with local Taliban leader Maulvi Nazir in early 2007, and the group worked with Baitullah Mehsud and the Tehrik-i-Taliban Pakistan. In April 2012 about 150 fighters of the movement and the Tehrik-i-Taliban attacked Bannu Prison, freeing nearly 400 prisoners. Its leader Usman Ghazi declared support for ISIS in September 2014, and videos in 2015 announced allegiance to Abu Bakr al-Baghdadi. In June 2016 a new faction denounced ISIS and declared loyalty to the Taliban and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">al-Qaeda</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Though founded as an ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> movement, it later recruited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajiks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a> and others. Of 87 martyrs listed on its website in 2011, only four were Uzbeks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. Its media arm was Jundallah Studio, which produced videos and statements in many languages. The movement's leaders after Yuldashev were Abu Usman Adil, killed in a drone strike in April 2012, and then Usman Ghazi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Islamic Movement of Uzbekistan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Islamist activity has risen slightly since 2015, with small groups such as the <b>Islamic Movement of Uzbekistan</b> declaring allegiance to Daesh, but the threat inside the country remains low.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan · Military</a></li></ul></section>
